WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.040
"Just How Bad Is Hospital Food?"

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:14.240
Hospital food needs a revolution.

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:16.960
I was surprised to find out
that most inpatient meals

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:20.080
served in hospitals are not
required to meet any sort of

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:22.320
national nutrition standards
for a healthy diet.

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:25.280
And indeed, an analysis on
the nutritional value of food

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:27.520
served to patients in
teaching hospitals found that

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:30.640
many did not meet
dietary recommendations.

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:35.040
“Warning,” read the headline,
“Hospital food bad for health.”

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:38.160
A registered dietitian wrote
in to defend the hospitals

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:40.880
saying, hey, at least
over half the hospitals met

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:42.800
at least over half the guidelines.

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:45.280
And hey, they’re so stringent anyway.

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:48.160
Little eggs and dairy, and you
could blow through your limits.

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:50.960
“The provocative conclusions
of [their analysis] only lead

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:52.800
the media and the public
to conclude that we are

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:55.360
a bunch of dunces who
have no understanding

00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:59.520
of the relation between nutrition
and disease prevention.”

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:04.080
Well, if the white coat fits…

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:05.960
Look, we spend a fortune
on training doctors;

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:08.560
you’d think we could
follow through on some

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:10.960
of the simplest things, like food.

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:13.920
“Good diet is as necessary
to recovery of health

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:16.880
as [anything else], and it is
folly to pretend that it is

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:19.120
beyond the power of our
profession to change this",

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:22.800
a line written 75 years ago

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:25.200
and still, there’s pushback.

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:27.840
“Perhaps, we should question
whether a ‘healthy diet’

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:30.560
given to a helpless patient
during a 2- to 10-day

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:32.880
hospital stay benefits anyone
or anything other than

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:35.440
the dietitian’s sense of ‘doing good’,”

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:38.480
responded one doctor, always bothered

00:01:38.480 --> 00:01:42.400
when patients are deprived
of a desired morning egg

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:45.120
because a “healthy”
diet has been ordered.

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:47.200
I mean, what’s a few days of a little

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:50.880
heart-unfriendly diet
in the scheme of things?

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:53.280
But it’s the message we’re sending.

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:55.440
“The presence of foods
on the [hospital] tray

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:58.080
sends a message to patients
as to what is healthy

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:00.640
and acceptable for them to eat,”
responded the researchers

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:03.600
that did the hospital foods analysis.

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:05.840
“We still can think of no
better place or opportunity

00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:08.080
to set an example of good nutrition

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:11.055
than when patients are in hospitals.”

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:14.320
After all, public schools
in California have banned

00:02:14.320 --> 00:02:17.280
the sale of soda for over a decade.

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:19.440
Why not children’s hospitals?

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:21.760
In a study of California
healthcare facilities

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:24.240
serving children, three-quarters of foods

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:26.960
and drinks wouldn’t have
been allowed in schools.

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:29.809
We’re talking soda, candy.

00:02:29.809 --> 00:02:31.920
Having unhealthy items
in healthcare facilities

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:34.720
and seeing staff consume
these products contradicts

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:37.680
the hypercritical nutrition
and health messages

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:40.960
children may get from
healthcare providers.

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:44.800
On adult menus, nearly all
meals contained excess salt,

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:47.680
with 100% of daily menus exceeding the

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:50.000
American Heart Association’s recommendation

00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:53.360
for staying under
1500 mg of sodium a day.

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:56.720
This means meals offered to
patients may actually contribute

00:02:56.720 --> 00:02:59.760
to the exacerbation
of the very conditions

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:03.120
that may have led them
to the hospitalization.

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:06.800
But if hospitals stuck to
the recommended limits of salt,

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:10.240
the food wouldn’t taste as good,
responded an executive

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:14.960
from the Salt Institute, to
which the researchers replied:

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:17.840
Taste as good? We’re talking
about hospital food—

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:21.215
it doesn’t taste good no matter
how much salt you put on it.

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:24.480
At the very least, we should
prepare all meals low sodium

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:27.520
and then, if they want to add
salt, it’s their choice.

00:03:27.520 --> 00:03:30.080
If they want to get
someone to wheel them out

00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:32.800
into the parking lot and smoke,
that’s their business,

00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:34.800
but we shouldn’t be
blowing cigarette smoke

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:36.560
into people’s rooms three times a day

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:39.294
whether they want it or not.

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:41.440
Interestingly, studies
suggest that when individuals

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:44.880
are allowed to salt food
to taste, they rarely add

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:48.480
as much as often becomes
prepackaged within the food.

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:52.480
Check it out. Switch people
to a low sodium diet and sure,

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:54.800
they use their saltshakers
more, but overall,

00:03:54.800 --> 00:03:57.520
their salt intake dips way down.

00:03:57.520 --> 00:04:00.640
And the study subjects
said it tasted just as salty,

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:03.120
because salt added to
the surface of foods

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:05.360
makes it taste saltier.

00:04:05.360 --> 00:04:08.400
But when a hospital meal
comes to you pre-salted

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:10.640
to the hilt, inpatients may not even have

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:14.880
the option to consume healthier levels.

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:17.839
In defense of their unhealthy food,
one hospital food service

00:04:17.839 --> 00:04:21.679
provider explained that they’re
just giving people what they want.

00:04:21.680 --> 00:04:24.320
Are we really going to deny
people who are going through

00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:28.080
difficult times any small
comfort they can get?

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:31.200
That’s one of the reasons why
this clinical director sends

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:35.040
candy bars and ice cream
to cancer patients.

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:37.760
You know what else might
help take the edge off?

00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:41.040
A nice long drag on a cigarette.

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:44.240
“Here you go Timmy, try this.”

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:46.320
Hospitals used to sell cigarettes,

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:48.880
primarily for "patient convenience."

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:51.360
“I don’t think I can deny
a paying patient the right

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:53.920
to smoke a cigarette,”
said one administrator,

00:04:53.920 --> 00:04:56.240
“I will have to insist we
have cigarette machines

00:04:56.240 --> 00:04:59.600
in the hospital, as a
service to the patient.”

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:03.440
But some made the radical
suggestion that tobacco products

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:05.120
should not be sold in the hospital.

00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:10.000
This wasn’t from the
1950’s, but from the 1980’s.

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:12.320
Yet, the irony of hospitals
allowing the sale

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:16.400
of a major cause of preventable
illness and death in this country

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:19.280
had rarely been discussed
in the medical literature,

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:22.064
and it’s especially ironic
that smoking was, of course,

00:05:22.064 --> 00:05:25.200
permitted in the doctors’ lounges.

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:28.320
To their credit, though,
U.S. hospitals underwent

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:30.560
the first industry-wide ban on smoking

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:32.400
in the workplace by the mid-90s.

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:35.360
Well, now “[h]ospitals
again have the opportunity

00:05:35.360 --> 00:05:38.080
to take the lead and
create food environments

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:40.800
that are consistent with
their mission to cure

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:43.520
the sick and to promote health.

00:05:43.520 --> 00:05:46.080
Through the simple act of
serving food that meets

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:49.040
national nutritional standards,
our hospitals will act

00:05:49.040 --> 00:05:50.880
in the best health
interests of their patients,

00:05:50.880 --> 00:05:54.320
and their staff, and will
undoubtedly again be leaders

00:05:54.320 --> 00:05:57.600
in our ongoing dialogue on
how to improve the food supply,

00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:02.720
which, in turn, will improve
the health of us all.”

00:06:02.720 --> 00:06:05.520
Strict antismoking regulations
were often criticized

00:06:05.520 --> 00:06:09.520
as being too harsh, as if
disease and premature death

00:06:09.520 --> 00:06:12.320
brought on by smoking
were any easier.

00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:15.680
Think my smoking-diet
parallel is hyperbole?

00:06:15.680 --> 00:06:20.800
Well, guess what. Today, the
major cause of preventable illness

00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:24.320
and death in this country
is no longer tobacco.

00:06:24.320 --> 00:06:30.560
The leading cause of death in
America is now the American diet.

00:06:30.560 --> 00:06:33.920
Hospitals in the United States
serve millions of patient meals

00:06:33.920 --> 00:06:40.154
each day and are optimally
positioned to model a healthy diet.

